{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aefdbf187ad9bd7a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9001-E001\n3\ttitle: Surface InferenceService deployment status in Dashboard model serving views\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9001\n5\tcomponent: Dashboard\n6\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Surface InferenceService deployment status in Dashboard model serving views\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers deployment status visibility for KServe\n34\tInferenceServices inside the RHOAI Dashboard. Data scientists will see a\n35\tcomputed status — Ready, Progressing, or Failed — for each deployed model in\n36\tthe model serving views, and can open a detail view showing the underlying\n37\tInferenceService condition messages and recent Kubernetes events for\n38\tdeployments that are failing or still progressing. The status shown reflects\n39\tthe actual InferenceService state within 30 seconds of a change, without a\n40\tmanual page reload, and the deployed models list can be filtered and sorted\n41\tby status.\n42\t\n43\tThe Dashboard reads InferenceService resources through the Kubernetes API\n44\t(the Dashboard owns no data plane); status is derived from the conditions and\n45\tevents KServe already exposes on the InferenceService resource. No KServe\n46\tcode changes are required.\n47\t\n48\t## Scope\n49\t\n50\t- Compute and display a deployment status (Ready / Progressing / Failed) per\n51\t  deployed model in the Dashboard model serving views.\n52\t- Status detail view for a deployment, showing the underlying InferenceService\n53\t  condition messages and recent events — used primarily for failed or\n54\t  progressing deployments.\n55\t- Keep displayed status within 30 seconds of the actual InferenceService state\n56\t  without requiring a manual page reload (the refresh mechanism — polling\n57\t  interval vs. Kubernetes watch — is an implementation decision for the\n58\t  Dashboard team).\n59\t- Filter and sort the deployed models list by status.\n60\t- Scope is limited to KServe InferenceService deployments; ModelMesh\n61\t  deployments, status alerting/notifications, and status history / timeline\n62\t  views are out of scope per the strategy.\n63\t\n64\t## Acceptance Criteria\n65\t\n66\t- Given a deployed model whose InferenceService is ready, the Dashboard model\n67\t  serving views show status Ready for it.\n68\t- Given a deployment that is progressing or failed, the views show\n69\t  Progressing or Failed respectively, and the displayed status matches the\n70\t  actual InferenceService state — measured by comparison against the\n71\t  InferenceService status conditions (e.g. Ready, PredictorReady) that KServe\n72\t  exposes (passive-component validation for KServe).\n73\t- Given a failed or progressing deployment, the user can open a status detail\n74\t  view showing the underlying condition messages and recent events for that\n75\t  deployment.\n76\t- Given a change in InferenceService state, the displayed status reflects the\n77\t  new state within 30 seconds, without a manual page reload.\n78\t- Given the deployed models list, the user can filter it by status and sort it\n79\t  by status.\n80\t- Automated tests cover status computation from InferenceService conditions\n81\t  and events for the Ready, Progressing, and Failed cases.\n82\t\n83\t## HLR Traceability\n84\t\n85\t- HLR-1 (P0): status per deployed model in model serving views — primary\n86\t  scope.\n87\t- HLR-2 (P0): status detail view with condition messages and recent events —\n88\t  primary scope.\n89\t- HLR-3 (P1): 30-second freshness without manual reload — primary scope.\n90\t- HLR-4 (P2): filter and sort by status — bundled here because it shares the\n91\t  deployed-models-list UI surface with HLR-1 and is not independently\n92\t  deferrable.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b90b85a90b67c200","agent_id":"a97b64c702410c015","session_id":"bcb47997-ec88-4b38-baa9-36155f1e5796","uuid":"e7c9fe29-2091-499b-824e-80ca82e49fc2","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:53:30.238Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aefdbf187ad9bd7a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9001 decomposition"}
