lues. Signal spot-checks against the rubric and strategy content: E001 `open_questions: 1` is justified (strategy explicitly says none blocking; polling-vs-watch does not change the approach), `repo_access: 1` (public odh-dashboard repos), `architecture_claims: 1` (strategy's condition claims corroborated by `kserve.md` and `dashboard.md` — verified present). E002 `human_process_gates: -1` is correct (Red Hat docs process includes technical review and editorial approval beyond ordinary PR review), and `open_questions: 0` is reasonable (content is settled by the completed implementation this epic depends on). No signal value contradicts the rubric conditions.\n30\t\n31\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n32\tE001 has six Given/When-style, testable ACs covering HLR-1 through HLR-4 plus automated tests for status computation; the KServe passive-component validation (displayed status must match actual InferenceService conditions) is folded in as an AC, matching the decomposition's stated handling. E002 includes the Rule 24-mandated \"technically reviewed against the implemented Dashboard status feature\" AC, plus publication via the Red Hat docs process. Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) does not apply — this is a new capability, not a replacement. Rule 25 (build pipeline green) does not apply — no konflux-onboarding epic exists in any dependency chain. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n33\t\n34\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n35\tAll strategy scope is covered: every HLR maps to an epic, the out-of-scope exclusions (ModelMesh, alerting, status history) are carried into E001's scope section, and HLR-2's condition messages and recent events are covered in both E001's scope and ACs. Strategy-level context is not silently dropped: the missing Impacted Teams table, the passive KServe component, the inferred docs team, and the unspecified freshness mechanism are all surfaced as health warnings rather than hidden. No conditional branches are needed (no bound